<h2>Indian masoor (red lentil) for export</h2>
<p>We supply masoor both whole (husk-on) and split (masoor dal), cleaned and graded to export tolerance. Our 2024 trade data shows a heavily Bangladesh-weighted trade — around 62% of both value and volume — with Iraq, Djibouti, the UAE, Malaysia and Sri Lanka making up most of the rest.</p>

<p>Madhya Pradesh is our primary sourcing state, with Uttar Pradesh, Bihar and West Bengal contributing volume. One honest note worth including: India is simultaneously a large lentil importer, since domestic demand often exceeds domestic production — so depending on the contract, export stock may be Indian-grown, imported-and-reprocessed, or a blend. We'll confirm the origin basis relevant to your order.</p>

<h2>Masoor export policy</h2>
<p>Masoor, like chickpeas, falls under the pulses category that DGFT moved from prohibited to Free export in September 2017 ("till further orders"), and that status has held since. See our <a href="/products/pulses/chickpeas/">chickpeas page</a> for the fuller policy history — the same background applies to masoor. We confirm current terms at the time of your order as standard practice across all products.</p>

<h2>How should I specify masoor?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form.</strong> Whole (husk-on) or split (dal) — different end uses, different price points.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture and cleanliness.</strong> 12–14% moisture and low foreign matter/admixture are the standard export tolerances.</li>
  <li><strong>Packing.</strong> 25kg/50kg bags for retail-adjacent buyers, jumbo bags for bulk millers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Masoor export pricing</h2>
